menuconfig AUDIO_PIPELINE
	bool "Zephyr audio pipeline"
	help
	  Enable the pull-based audio pipeline subsystem.

	  This symbol brings in the pipeline core only - the thread, the frame
	  buffer, the event queue and the node dispatch. The nodes shipped with
	  the subsystem are separate symbols under "Nodes" below, so an image
	  carries the ones the application wires up with a *_NODE_DEFINE() macro
	  and nothing else. In particular FILE_SYSTEM is selected by the two file
	  nodes that actually talk to it, not by the pipeline: a target with no
	  storage - the first hardware targets of this module have none - would
	  otherwise pay for a filesystem dispatch layer it can never use.

if AUDIO_PIPELINE

menu "Nodes"

config AUDIO_PIPELINE_NODE_FILE_READER
	bool "File reader source node"
	select FILE_SYSTEM
	help
	  Source node that reads a RIFF/WAVE file through the Zephyr filesystem
	  API. That API is why this symbol, and not AUDIO_PIPELINE, selects
	  FILE_SYSTEM. The select only pulls in the dispatch layer: the
	  application still chooses a filesystem type (ext2, FAT, littlefs, ...)
	  and mounts it before opening the pipeline.

	  Defaults to n like every node symbol here. A node is only reachable
	  through its *_NODE_DEFINE() macro, so an application always knows
	  which nodes it uses and can say so; the reverse - a default the
	  application never asked for - is text nobody can point at. For this
	  node it is also a whole subsystem nobody asked for.

config AUDIO_PIPELINE_NODE_FILE_WRITER
	bool "File writer sink node"
	select FILE_SYSTEM
	help
	  Sink node that writes the stream to a RIFF/WAVE file through the
	  Zephyr filesystem API. As with the reader, the select lives here
	  rather than on AUDIO_PIPELINE because this node is one of the two that
	  need it, and it only pulls in the dispatch layer - the application
	  mounts a filesystem itself.

	  Defaults to n for the same reason as the reader: nodes are opted into
	  explicitly, so the set of nodes in an image is visible in prj.conf.

config AUDIO_PIPELINE_NODE_GAIN_FILTER
	bool "Gain filter node"
	help
	  Filter node that scales every sample by a Q15 gain. Pure arithmetic,
	  no dependencies.

	  Defaults to n so that the node set is opted into explicitly, like
	  every other node symbol here, rather than being cheap enough that
	  nobody notices it. Uniformity is the point: one rule for all four
	  shipped nodes is easier to reason about than a size threshold.

config AUDIO_PIPELINE_NODE_NULL_SINK
	bool "Null sink node"
	help
	  Sink node that discards every frame it is handed. Useful to terminate
	  a chain while bringing a source or a filter up, and it keeps no state.

	  Defaults to n so that the node set is opted into explicitly, like
	  every other node symbol here.

endmenu

config AUDIO_PIPELINE_FRAME_SAMPLES
	int "Samples per frame"
	default 128
	range 1 1024
	help
	  Frame size processed by the pipeline thread in one iteration.

config AUDIO_PIPELINE_EVENT_QUEUE_DEPTH
	int "Event queue depth per pipeline"
	default 4
	range 1 32
	help
	  Number of audio_pipeline_event entries the per-pipeline message queue
	  can hold. The storage is allocated statically, either by
	  AUDIO_PIPELINE_DEFINE() or - for a zero-initialised instance - by the
	  subsystem itself. When the queue is full the newest event is dropped,
	  so the oldest ones (and with them the first error) always survive.

config AUDIO_PIPELINE_THREAD_PRIO
	int "Pipeline thread priority"
	default 5
	help
	  Priority assigned to the pipeline worker thread.

config AUDIO_PIPELINE_THREAD_STACK_SIZE
	int "Pipeline thread stack size"
	default 2048
	help
	  Stack size for the pipeline worker thread.

endif
